# Security Considerations Of This Project 

This project was a learning exercise so there were a few security considerations when making the scanner to ensure best practices. 

## What the scanner actually checks
The Lambda function only checks one thing: whether each S3 bucket has
default server-side encryption configured (`AES256` or `aws:kms`). It does
not check bucket policies, public access settings, versioning, logging, or
any other S3 security control. A bucket could pass this scan and still be
publicly readable (i.e. encryption at rest and public access are
separate settings in S3 and not included in the scan)

## Handling the Gemini API key
The API key is stored as a Lambda environment variable rather than being
hardcoded in `src/s3_scanner.py` or committed to this repository. Lambda
environment variables are encrypted at rest by AWS, which is why this
pattern (read the key with `os.environ.get(...)`) is used instead of a
plaintext config file. `.env.example` documents the variable name without
containing a real key, and `.env` is excluded via `.gitignore` in case a
local copy is ever created for testing.

## Least-privilege IAM roles 
The Lambda execution role (`LambdaS3ScannerRole`) is scoped narrowly on
purpose:

- `s3:ListAllMyBuckets` and `s3:GetEncryptionConfiguration` are the only S3
  permissions granted, and both are read-only. The function can't modify,
  delete, or create anything in S3.
- `AWSLambdaBasicExecutionRole` is the only other policy attached, and it
  only allows writing to CloudWatch Logs.

This means that even if the Lambda code or its dependencies were somehow
compromised, the impact is limited to reading bucket names and
encryption settings and not other roles. 

## Known limitations
- The scan results and AI analysis currently only go to CloudWatch Logs.
  There's no alerting (email, Slack, etc.) if unencrypted buckets are
  found, someone has to go and check the logs. This is a consideration as
  part of my future implementations.
- The function doesn't take any remediation action (such as enabling
  encryption automatically) and it only reports.
- Gemini's analysis is generated text, not a guaranteed-accurate security
  audit. It is used to convert raw text into plain english for understanding 
  but cannot be fully relied upon.
